<rss xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom" xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/" version="2.0"><channel><title>search – justaddwater.dk</title><link>https://watson.github.io/justaddwater.dk/pr-preview/pr-1/tag/search/</link><description>Instant Usability &amp;amp; Web Standards — static archive</description><language>en-US</language><atom:link href="https://watson.github.io/justaddwater.dk/pr-preview/pr-1/tag/search/feed/" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml" /><item><title>Chrome “find in page” with smart search in unicode characters</title><link>https://watson.github.io/justaddwater.dk/pr-preview/pr-1/2011/09/29/chrome-find-in-page-with-smart-search-in-unicode-characters/</link><guid isPermaLink="false">http://justaddwater.dk/?p=1512</guid><dc:creator>Jesper Rønn-Jensen</dc:creator><pubDate>Thu, 29 Sep 2011 20:42:50 +0000</pubDate><description>&lt;p&gt;Look at this chrome search for the Danish letter “æ” in a page.&lt;br/&gt;
&lt;a href="https://watson.github.io/justaddwater.dk/pr-preview/pr-1/wp-content/uploads/2011/09/Unicode-UTF-8-character-table-chrome-search-for-danish-letter-%C3%A6.png"&gt;&lt;img alt="" class="aligncenter size-medium wp-image-1511" height="76" sizes="(max-width: 300px) 100vw, 300px" src="https://watson.github.io/justaddwater.dk/pr-preview/pr-1/wp-content/uploads/2011/09/Unicode-UTF-8-character-table-chrome-search-for-danish-letter-%C3%A6-300x76.png" srcset="https://watson.github.io/justaddwater.dk/pr-preview/pr-1/wp-content/uploads/2011/09/Unicode-UTF-8-character-table-chrome-search-for-danish-letter-%C3%A6-300x76.png 300w, https://watson.github.io/justaddwater.dk/pr-preview/pr-1/wp-content/uploads/2011/09/Unicode-UTF-8-character-table-chrome-search-for-danish-letter-%C3%A6.png 688w" title="Unicode-UTF-8-character-table-chrome-search-for-danish-letter-æ" width="300"/&gt;&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Notice how Chrome actually also shows occurrences of “ae” when searching for “æ”.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The same thing goes when searching for “Ø”, “Ö” or similar accents: They will show occurences of “o”.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Usability wise, I think this is a rather good principle for end-user solutions, just like Google Chrome. But, but. As an advanced user, i would like to know if i can turn it off? Not as a general setting. Because the usecase would be that you search, and find too much. Based on this, if you search for something with accented characters, a new checkbox should appear. My suggestion (mockup):&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://watson.github.io/justaddwater.dk/pr-preview/pr-1/wp-content/uploads/2011/09/Unicode-UTF-8-character-table-chrome-search-for-danish-letter-%C3%A6-loose-search.png"&gt;&lt;img alt="" class="aligncenter size-medium wp-image-1514" height="87" sizes="(max-width: 300px) 100vw, 300px" src="https://watson.github.io/justaddwater.dk/pr-preview/pr-1/wp-content/uploads/2011/09/Unicode-UTF-8-character-table-chrome-search-for-danish-letter-%C3%A6-loose-search-300x87.png" srcset="https://watson.github.io/justaddwater.dk/pr-preview/pr-1/wp-content/uploads/2011/09/Unicode-UTF-8-character-table-chrome-search-for-danish-letter-%C3%A6-loose-search-300x87.png 300w, https://watson.github.io/justaddwater.dk/pr-preview/pr-1/wp-content/uploads/2011/09/Unicode-UTF-8-character-table-chrome-search-for-danish-letter-%C3%A6-loose-search.png 558w" title="Unicode-UTF-8-character-table-chrome-search-for-danish-letter-æ-loose-search" width="300"/&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This way, you will start with a “wide” search and end up being able to filter in more correctly.&lt;/p&gt;
</description></item></channel></rss>